data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1792058852818,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin crewed the mission, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three parts: the command module, which housed the astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module, providing propulsion, power, oxygen, and water; and the Lunar Module.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The Lunar Module had a descent stage with a large engine and fuel tanks for landing, and an ascent stage with a cabin for two astronauts and a small engine.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC, while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard the CM Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong was the first human to walk on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The crew spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"After over 21 hours on the surface, Armstrong and Aldrin rejoined Collins in lunar orbit, and the crew splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 fulfilled a goal set by President John F. Kennedy in May 1961 to land a man on the Moon before the decade ended.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union, and the program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong declared, \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind,\\\" upon stepping onto the lunar surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"The Sputnik crisis ignited the Space Race, as both superpowers sought to demonstrate superiority in spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Space Race\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy addressed Congress on \\\"Urgent National Needs\\\" and called for landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"mankind\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says reaching the Moon will be a national effort, since all of us must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argues outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its conquest deserves the best of all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy asks why the Moon is the goal, comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy's refrain declares 'We choose to go to the Moon' as the nation's deliberate decision.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Rice University football stadium\",\"Texas\",\"the Moon\",\"outer space\",\"mankind\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy declared the nation would go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard, and the goal would organize and measure the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle\\\".\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon expedition again at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the joint mission idea was later abandoned after his death.\"},{\"id\":\"7.6\",\"text\":\"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module had a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The service module supplied the command module with propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had descent and ascent stages, allowing a single Saturn V launch and enabling Apollo's lunar landing design.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"MOSFETs\",\"Silicon integrated circuit (IC) chips\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"Project Apollo was abruptly halted by the Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967, which killed Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit in October 1968.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit in December 1968.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 9 put the lunar module through its paces in Earth orbit in March 1969.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit in May 1969, leaving all ready for Apollo 11's Moon landing by July 1969.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"mankind\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Rice University football stadium\",\"Texas\",\"the Moon\",\"outer space\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"MOSFETs\",\"Silicon integrated circuit (IC) chips\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"15a91300a38444f7747d8398dd5e1e2085eba3dffc73794f92ec3806d84f1449"}}

