d at the National Air and Space Museum."}]},{"chunk":4,"points":[{"id":"4.1","text":"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities."},{"id":"4.2","text":"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority."},{"id":"4.3","text":"The Sputnik crisis ignited the Space Race, as both superpowers sought to demonstrate superiority in spaceflight."},{"id":"4.4","text":"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit."},{"id":"4.5","text":"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital."}]},{"chunk":5,"points":[{"id":"5.1","text":"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position."},{"id":"5.2","text":"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose."},{"id":"5.3","text":"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy addressed Congress on \"Urgent National Needs\" and called for landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end."},{"id":"5.4","text":"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive."},{"id":"5.5","text":"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration."}]},{"chunk":6,"points":[{"id":"6.1","text":"Kennedy says reaching the Moon will be a national effort, since all of us must work to put one man there."},{"id":"6.2","text":"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football sta